  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The Social Security Administration (SSA) warns of a surge in scams where impersonators threaten to suspend Social Security numbers (SSN) to steal money or personal information. Key red flags include demands for immediate payment via gift cards, crypto, or wire transfers, and claims of problems with your SSN. If you receive a suspicious call, hang up immediately. Common Scam Tactics Impersonation: Scammers pretend to be SSA or Office of the Inspector General (OIG) agents. Threats: Threatening arrest or legal action for fake issues with your account. Phishing Emails/Texts: Emails with links claiming to be \"Claim Benefits!\" or that your account is suspended. Identity Theft: Using fabricated letters or emails to gather your personal information and redirect benefits. How to Protect Yourself SSA Never Calls to Demand Payment: The SSA will never threaten you, demand immediate payment, or ask for credit card numbers over the phone. Never Pay with Gift Cards: Do not pay with gift cards, prepaid debit cards, cryptocurrency, or by mailing cash. Verify the Source: If a call seems suspicious, hang up and call the official SSA hotline at 1-800-772-1213. Guard Information: Do not share personal information (bank details, SSN) if you did not initiate the contact. Where to Report Scams OIG Website: Report scams to the SSA Office of the Inspector General. SSA Office of the Inspector General FTC: File a report at IdentityTheft.gov. IdentityTheft.gov Note: The SSA will generally send a letter if there is a real issue with your account.",
